Original commit body from cfc6980bbee4cf442c27e04650b5aadf561c19c9:

`t = getenv(); n = acc.push(t); log(n); send(acc[0])` was a false clean in
JS and Python: the walk followed the heir `n`, a terminating `log` closed it,
and the walk returned False, which removes the flow, while the secret went
into `acc`.

The use line WAS asked the escape question, but the walk's per-line call
index (`_WalkIndexes.callee_names`) held only calls with a catalogue key. An
unresolved `acc.push` and every resolved first-party call (WI-zumud gate)
were absent, so the line read as "no call at this line at all", the one
permitting case that skips the question. The no-heir branch had the same
hole: `log(acc.push(t))` read as all-terminating.

A taint call edge with no catalogue key now adds UNCATALOGUED_CALL to the
index at its sites. No summary is keyed by it, so it never closes a branch:
the rule `_source_line_escapes` already applied to the source's own line
(WI-jusoj b). One index fix covers all four walk sites. Direction: only
False -> None, so findings can only be kept, never removed.

